    "question": "A defense contractor operating in the aerospace sector must align its corporate governance and vendor vetting programs with the Department of Defense (DoD) Cybersecurity Maturity Model Certification (CMMC) 2.0 framework, specifically Level 2 requirements for protecting Controlled Unclassified Information (CUI). During a comprehensive supply chain audit, the security director discovers that a critical Tier-2 sub-contractor utilizes a foreign-owned cloud service provider for data backup. What specific compliance breaches have occurred relative to DFARS Clause 252.204-7012, and what exact legal and operational remediation workflows must be executed to protect the prime contractor from contract termination or False Claims Act litigation?",
    "answer": "The primary compliance breach stems from a failure to meet the security requirements codified in DFARS Clause 252.204-7012 and NIST SP 800-171, which govern the protection of CUI. Specifically, DFARS mandates that any cloud service provider utilized by a contractor or sub-contractor to store, process, or transmit CUI must meet security requirements equivalent to the Federal Risk and Authorization Management Program (FedRAMP) Moderate baseline. Furthermore, the use of a foreign-owned cloud service introduces severe risks regarding data sovereignty, foreign intelligence surveillance access, and compliance with International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR) restrictions. Remediation requires immediate, high-priority operational and legal workflows. Operationally, the prime contractor must direct the sub-contractor to halt all transfers of CUI to the unapproved foreign cloud environment, isolate the compromised data, and migrate all backup operations to a FedRAMP Moderate (or higher) authorized, US-sovereign cloud platform. Legally, the prime contractor must review its mandatory reporting obligations; under DFARS 252.204-7012, a cyber incident involving potential CUI compromise must be reported to the DoD Chief Information Officer within 72 hours of discovery. To mitigate exposure under the False Claims Act—where a company can face treble damages for knowingly misrepresenting its compliance status—the contractor must fully document the breach, the immediate corrective action plan, and voluntarily disclose the systemic breakdown and remediation timeline to the contracting officer.",
