    "answer": "The designated corporate Incident Commander must establish the ICP at a safe, secure location outside the immediate hot zone, ensuring it is positioned upwind and uphill from any potential IED blast radius or chemical off-gassing, while remaining accessible to arriving first responders. Upon arrival of public safety agencies, the corporate Incident Commander must immediately transition the management structure into a Unified Command framework. In this structure, the corporate security director co-locates with the NYPD tactical commander and FDNY battalion chief, sharing a single command post to synchronize floor plans, surveillance feeds, and building access control maps without relinquishing internal corporate communication responsibilities. Emergency communications must follow a pre-planned, tightly controlled protocol to prevent panic and protect operational security. Internal alerts to guests and employees must use clear, objective, and directive language via the mass notification system (e.g., instructing specific sectors to run, hide, or fight based on real-time threat tracking), avoiding coded jargon that causes confusion. External communications must be routed exclusively through a single Public Information Officer (PIO) assigned to the Unified Command structure. To safeguard tactical law enforcement operations, the PIO must refrain from broadcasting specific tactical positions, SWAT staging points, or unverified casualty counts over public channels or social media, thereby preventing the adversaries from monitoring response movements via open-source channels.",
