    "answer": "Standard 1/2-inch laminated glass mounted in commercial-grade aluminum framing fails quickly under mechanical attack because hand tools like sledgehammers or prybars can rapidly shatter the glass matrix and distort the soft aluminum frames, causing the entire glazing unit to pop out of its track within seconds. To achieve a certified 15-minute delay against forced entry, the entire fenestration assembly must be upgraded to meet the rigorous testing metrics of ASTM F1233 (Standard Test Method for Security Glazing Materials and Systems) or ASTM F3038 (Standard Test Method for Timed Evaluation of Forced-Entry Resistance for Security Systems). The retrofit requires replacing the standard glass with a heavy-duty, multi-layered polycarbonate-glass composite glazing material. This material should feature alternating layers of high-tensile polycarbonate and polyurethane bonding sheets designed to absorb and dissipate repetitive mechanical impacts without breaching. Concurrently, the soft aluminum framing must be replaced with heavy-gauge, structural steel-reinforced framing units that are securely anchored into the surrounding structural masonry using high-strength epoxy shear bolts. This prevents the frame from buckling or being physically pried away from the building envelope during a sustained attack.",
