    "answer": "To prevent claims of false imprisonment or coercion under civil tort law, the investigator must meticulously structure both the environment and the interaction. The physical layout must ensure that the employee’s seat is positioned closest to the exit door, and the door must remain unlocked at all times, ensuring an unblocked path out of the room. At the start of the interview, the investigator must deliver a clear, unambiguous verbal admonition stating that the interview is completely voluntary, that the employee is free to leave at any moment, and that they can choose to stop answering questions at any point. In a purely private capacity, employees do not possess Fifth Amendment Miranda rights during internal corporate interviews, and private employers can legally terminate employment for a refusal to cooperate under the employment-at-will doctrine. However, if the firm is operating under a direct federal mandate or in joint tandem with a federal agency (such as a defense contractor acting under explicit instructions from the DCIS or FBI), the private investigator’s actions may be construed as \"state action.\" Under this scenario, if the employee is subjected to custodial interrogation without appropriate constitutional protections or coerced into making self-incriminating statements under the threat of immediate termination, those statements could be suppressed in a subsequent criminal prosecution under the Garrity or Calcandra doctrines.",
