    "question": "A critical chemical manufacturing facility subject to the Department of Homeland Security’s Chemical Facility Anti-Terrorism Standards (CFATS) is updating its Site Security Plan (SSP) to satisfy Risk-Based Performance Standards (RBPS) 11 (Security Lighting). The facility's perimeter fence borders a dense, environmentally protected wetland. The physical security architect must calculate the exact illumination levels required to support intelligent video analytics for automated intrusion detection while preventing light pollution into the wetland. How do you balance the trade-offs between Lux levels, Color Rendering Index (CRI), and infrared (IR) illumination to optimize detection probability?",
    "answer": "Balancing these parameters requires matching lighting specifications with the operational requirements of the video analytics engine. To minimize environmental impact on the wetland, high-intensity visible light pollution must be strictly curbed, ruling out continuous high-Lux broad-spectrum white lighting at the outer fence line. Instead, the architect should establish a baseline visible light level of approximately 2 to 5 Lux at ground level using targeted, full-cutoff LED fixtures with a high Color Rendering Index (CRI $\\ge 80$). A high CRI ensures that the analytic algorithms can accurately differentiate spectral signatures, improving target classification (e.g., distinguishing a human in camouflage from blowing foliage). To maximize intrusion detection probability without disturbing the wetland ecology, the primary surveillance layer must rely on high-power, covert or semi-covert Infrared (IR) illuminators (typically operating at the 850nm or 940nm wavelength) paired with specialized day/night security cameras. IR illumination provides the required sensor contrast for the analytics engine to trip virtual line-crossings and pixel-change thresholds in complete darkness. To optimize this setup, the video analytics should be calibrated to account for the lack of color data in the IR spectrum by relying heavily on spatial geometry, velocity thresholds, and edge-detection algorithms to maintain a high Probability of Detection ($P_d$) while minimizing False Alarm Rates ($FAR$).",
