    "question": "A critical infrastructure site, such as an LNG export terminal on the Gulf Coast, is refining its physical protection system (PPS) to mitigate the threat of airborne Unmanned Aircraft Systems (UAS) carrying explosive payloads. The security director is evaluating counter-UAS (C-UAS) technologies, specifically RF jamming systems and kinetic interception solutions. Given the strict regulatory oversight of the Federal Communications Commission (FCC) and the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) within the United States, what specific legal and operational constraints dictate the deployment of active jamming versus passive detection technologies for commercial enterprise security?",
    "answer": "The deployment of active RF jamming systems by private commercial enterprises is strictly illegal under federal law. The Communications Act of 1934, enforced by the FCC, prohibits the willful interference or jamming of radio communications, including the command-and-control frequencies used by UAS. Furthermore, the FAA views an active drone as an aircraft; intercepting, disabling, or shooting down a drone via kinetic means or electronic hijacking violates 18 U.S.C. § 32 (Destruction of Aircraft), carrying severe federal criminal penalties regardless of whether the drone is trespassing over private critical infrastructure. Consequently, commercial enterprise security must rely on passive detection and tracking technologies. These include multi-channel RF sensors that intercept and decode the signal signatures between the drone and the operator, acoustic sensors that identify the unique sound profile of drone rotors, and long-range optical/thermal cameras. These passive systems provide early warning and telemetry data without violating FCC or FAA statutes. For physical mitigation, commercial operators must focus on passive hardening measures. These include installing overhead blast netting, deploying structural reinforcement over critical infrastructure components, and coordinating real-time tracking data directly with local law enforcement agencies authorized to engage under federal federal law enforcement exceptions.",
