    "id": 33,
    "question": "A private investigator is retained by a major US pharmaceutical firm to investigate a corporate espionage leak involving a senior scientist suspected of selling synthetic drug formulas to a domestic competitor. The investigator surreptitiously installs a hardware keylogger onto the scientist's company-issued desktop workstation and configures a hidden camera inside the office ceiling tile to monitor their movements. What specific statutory restrictions under the Electronic Communications Privacy Act (ECPA) and state-level workplace privacy common laws apply to this investigative scenario, and how must evidence be managed to preserve admissibility in federal civil litigation?",
    "answer": "The deployment of the hardware keylogger and the hidden ceiling camera on corporate property must be analyzed under the Electronic Communications Privacy Act (ECPA) and common-law privacy torts like intrusion upon seclusion. Under the ECPA, capturing keystrokes can be legally executed by a private employer on company-owned infrastructure, provided the employer has established an explicit corporate monitoring policy. This policy must state that employees have no expectation of privacy when using company hardware and network assets, thereby securing implied consent under 18 U.S.C. § 2511(2)(d). The hidden ceiling camera introduces significant civil liability for intrusion upon seclusion if installed in areas where employees have a reasonable expectation of privacy, such as locker rooms or private restrooms. However, an individual’s private workspace or open office cubicle generally carries a reduced expectation of privacy, particularly when notice of general video surveillance has been disseminated. To ensure the collected electronic evidence is admissible in a federal civil trial under the Federal Rules of Evidence (FRE), the investigator must document a sterile, continuous forensic collection workflow. This requires validating that the keylogger logs and video files are hashed immediately upon extraction and that their physical media handles are continuously logged on an unyielding chain-of-custody record.",
