    "id": 34,
    "question": "A security systems integration engineer is designing a comprehensive personnel access control system for a financial center. The facility requires implementing a dual-factor authentication matrix (smart card plus biometric iris recognition) across 45 ingress points. When designing the network topology connecting these edge door controllers to the centralized Access Control Server, what specific network security protocols and cryptographic baselines must be enforced to protect edge transmissions from man-in-the-middle (MitM) credential harvesting attacks?",
    "answer": "To protect edge-to-server communications from man-in-the-middle (MitM) attacks, packet sniffing, and credential replay vectors, the network architecture must eliminate legacy unencrypted protocols like Wiegand at the edge and standard HTTP at the network layer. The edge reader-to-controller wiring must utilize the Open Supervised Device Protocol (OSDP) verified to Secure Channel Profile standards, which enforces AES-128 or AES-256 encryption directly between the card reader and the local controller panel. This prevents adversaries from physically tapping the reader wiring to harvest raw credential data. From the local controller panel to the centralized Access Control Server, all IP-based network traffic must be encapsulated using Transport Layer Security (TLS 1.3) with strong cryptographic cipher suites, such as ECDHE-RSA-AES256-GCM-SHA384. This ensures mutual authentication via digital certificates between the server and every edge panel. Any attempt by an unauthorized device to intercept or inject packets onto the access control subnet will cause a cryptographic handshake failure, immediately isolating the port and triggering a high-priority security alarm while keeping the underlying credential data completely protected against decryption.",
    "topic": "Physical Security"
