    "id": 108,
    "question": "An enterprise security architect is integrating a perimeter intrusion detection system (PIDS) utilizing fiber-optic strain sensors on a chain-link fence paired with outdoor dual-technology microwave and passive infrared (PIR) sensors. How should the alarm logic gates be configured at the software level to optimize the system for an environment prone to high winds and frequent wildlife crossings, without sacrificing the probability of detection ($P_D$)?",
    "answer": "The fence-mounted fiber-optic sensors and the volumetric ground-based sensors (microwave/PIR) should be configured using an \"AND\" logic gate configuration for automated alarm generation, but backed by a dynamic \"OR\" queuing logic for human verification. For the ground sensors, the microwave (which detects mass motion via Doppler shift) and the PIR (which detects changes in thermal radiation) must operate in an \"AND\" configuration to prevent false alarms from wind-blown debris or thermal fluctuations. When combining the fence line with the volumetric sensors, requiring a simultaneous or closely sequenced trigger within a specific time window (e.g., 5 seconds) filters out wildlife crossings or wind rattling the fence, maintaining a high $P_D$ for actual human climbing and breaching while driving down nuisance alarm rates (NAR).",
