    "id": 124,
    "question": "A corporate security team is managing an active hostage and barricade situation at a commercial office building. The threat actor is a disgruntled former employee armed with a firearm. As the corporate security director coordinating with the local law enforcement tactical response team and crisis negotiation unit (CNU), what is the primary role of the corporate security force during the stabilization phase of the incident?",
    "answer": "The primary role of the corporate security force is to act as a critical information enabler and logistical coordinator for law enforcement command, rather than executing tactical interventions. Corporate security must immediately provide responding tactical units with detailed, up-to-date architectural blueprints, master access control credentials (smart cards/keys) to bypass locked doors, live feeds and control of the CCTV system, and comprehensive dossiers on the threat actor (including HR files, photos, and psychological background). Additionally, corporate security must isolate the building's mechanical controls (HVAC/elevators) at the tactical commander's direction, assist in locking down adjacent uncompromised sectors, and secure the perimeter to prevent media or bystanders from interfering with operations.",
    "topic": "Crisis Management & BCP"
