    "id": 125,
    "question": "An industrial manufacturing facility uses an anhydrous ammonia storage tank inside a fenced compound. The security manager is implementing a physical protection system to comply with the Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) Process Safety Management (PSM) standard and EPA Risk Management Plan (RMP) rules. What specific category of electronic sensor should be deployed to detect an intentional breach of the tank valve itself, and how must it be integrated into the central monitoring console?",
    "answer": "To detect an intentional breach or unauthorized manipulation of the tank valve, a combination of valve position supervisory switches (high-security tamper switches) and localized toxic gas detection sensors (electrochemical or infrared gas detectors) must be deployed directly at the asset valve assembly. These sensors must be wired into an intrinsically safe, explosion-proof enclosure to satisfy National Electrical Code (NEC) Class I, Division 1 hazardous location standards. The signals must be integrated into the central security management system via a supervised, dual-end-of-line (EOL) resistor network, ensuring that any attempt to cut, short, or tamper with the sensor wiring triggers an immediate high-priority alarm at the security console, completely independent of standard industrial process telemetry.",
