    "id": 126,
    "question": "A private university campus is implementing an upgraded emergency mass notification system (EMNS) to comply with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act (Clery Act). During an active shooter incident, the system fails to send out an alert to 40% of the student body due to a database sync error with the telecom provider. What are the legal, regulatory, and financial implications for the institution under federal law?",
    "answer": "Under the Clery Act, institutions must issue an immediate \"timely warning\" or \"emergency notification\" to the entire campus community upon confirmation of a significant danger or emergency involving an immediate threat to health or safety. A systematic failure that leaves 40% of the population unnotified constitutes a serious regulatory violation. The US Department of Education can conduct a compliance audit, leading to substantial civil monetary penalties per individual violation (fines are adjusted annually for inflation, reaching over $\\$60,000$ per violation). Furthermore, the institution faces immense civil liability exposure through negligence lawsuits filed by victims or their families, reputational damage resulting in reduced enrollment, and the potential loss of eligibility for federal student financial aid programs.",
    "topic": "Security Risk Management"
