    "id": 127,
    "question": "A security engineer is designing a defense-in-depth physical security posture for an executive boardroom where highly confidential merger and acquisition strategies are negotiated. The engineer must mitigate the threat of technical surveillance countermeasures (TSCM) failures, specifically laser-microphone eavesdropping targeting the exterior windows. What physical and material modifications must be applied to the boardroom windows to defeat this interception vector?",
    "answer": "To defeat laser-microphone eavesdropping, which operates by measuring the microscopic vibrations caused by acoustic sound waves hitting window glass, the physical structure of the windows must be modified. The facility should install heavy-duty, multi-layered laminated glass windows with a thick polyvinyl butyral (PVB) or acoustic interlayer, which dampens glass vibrations. Furthermore, the exterior panes should be fitted with specialized window films containing micro-thin metallic coatings that disrupt the reflection of infrared laser beams. Alternatively, acoustic window jammers (transducers connected to a white-noise masking generator) should be mounted directly onto the glass surfaces to inject random kinetic vibrations, completely scrambling any acoustic signals the laser microphone attempts to decode from the reflected beam.",
    "topic": "Physical Security"
