    "id": 128,
    "question": "A secure logistics company transports high-value physical cryptocurrency cold-storage wallets between commercial vaults via armored transport vehicles. The security division must evaluate the threat of a rolling ambush by a criminal syndicate utilizing radio-frequency jamming devices designed to disrupt cellular, GPS, and satellite communications. What specific operational protocol and technical hardware countermeasure should be implemented to ensure constant tracking and communications capability?",
    "answer": "To counter wide-spectrum RF jamming, the armored vehicles must be equipped with specialized multi-band, automated frequency-hopping spread spectrum (FHSS) radio systems and vehicular satellite communication terminals operating on highly resilient military or commercial bands (such as X-band or Ka-band) with directional, anti-jamming antenna arrays. Operationally, the security division must implement a strict \"dead-man\" tracking protocol coupled with automated geofencing. If the vehicle's telemetry signal drops completely out of coverage for a predetermined window (e.g., 60 seconds), the central Security Operations Center (SOC) must immediately declare an active emergency event, activate pre-coordinated local law enforcement tactical response protocols along the route, and remotely trigger the armored vehicle's internal secondary locking and fuel-cutoff systems.",
    "topic": "Crisis Management & BCP"
