    "question": "A high-end technology manufacturer discovers that counterfeit components are entering its domestic assembly lines, resulting in critical hardware failures. The corporate investigator traces the source to an approved third-party supplier who is secretly substituting substandard parts. To protect corporate intellectual property and minimize supply chain liability, what legal remedies and security controls should be executed simultaneously?",
    "answer": "The organization must immediately issue a formal cease-and-desist letter, suspend the supplier's contract under breach of contract clauses, and file for an emergency injunction in federal court to seize counterfeit inventory under the Trademark Counterfeiting Act. Simultaneously, security controls must be upgraded to an end-to-end cryptographic supply chain verification framework. This involves deploying tamper-evident blockchain-based serialization or unique component tagging (such as microscopic chemical DNA markers or cryptographic microchips) on all genuine source parts. Incoming quality assurance protocols must be integrated with the security access control system, ensuring that components from the suspended supplier are automatically flagged, quarantined in a secure storage zone, and subjected to destructive testing prior to line introduction.",
