    "id": 132,
    "question": "A commercial high-rise building in New York City is retrofitting its physical security infrastructure. The facility manager must install a series of architectural glass turnstiles in the main lobby to manage tenant access control. To comply with both building security standards and life safety codes, specifically NFPA 101 (Life Safety Code) and the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA), how must the turnstile array be physically and electronically engineered?",
    "answer": "The turnstile array must feature at least one dedicated lane that complies with ADA width requirements (minimum 32 inches of clear passage) to accommodate wheelchairs. Electronically, the entire turnstile system must be integrated directly with the building’s master fire alarm control panel (FACP). Under NFPA 101, upon activation of a fire alarm or loss of primary electrical power, the turnstile barriers must automatically fail-safe to the completely open position, allowing unobstructed egress for evacuating occupants. The manual override buttons must also be installed at a monitored security desk within direct line of sight of the turnstiles, ensuring security personnel can instantly release the barriers in an emergency.",
