    "id": 136,
    "question": "A private security contractor is hired to provide maritime security for commercial cargo vessels transiting the High Risk Area (HRA) in the Indian Ocean. The security team leader must develop the Rules for the Use of Force (RUF) regarding defensive engagements with maritime pirates. Under international maritime law, specifically the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS) and the absolute authority of the ship’s master, what is the legal hierarchy governing the deployment of lethal force by the security team?",
    "answer": "The absolute legal authority on a commercial vessel rests with the Ship’s Master (Captain), regardless of the presence of an embarked private security team. The security team leader acts as a tactical advisor to the Master. Lethal force can only be deployed in compliance with the established RUF, which must align with the laws of the vessel’s Flag State, the coastal state guidelines, and international law principles of self-defense and proportionality. The security team cannot independently initiate a lethal engagement; they must operate under the overarching authority of the Master, escalating force through visible deterrents, non-lethal warning flares, and disabling fire only when an imminent threat to human life is clearly demonstrated.",
