    "id": 140,
    "question": "A major energy corporation is upgrading the physical security of its electrical transmission substations in alignment with the NERC CIP-014 standard. The standard mandates a comprehensive vulnerability assessment and the implementation of a security plan to verify protection against physical attacks. If the threat assessment identifies high-powered rifle fire from a distance of 100 yards as a primary risk vector for the substation transformers, what structural defensive barrier material and configuration should be deployed to mitigate this risk?",
    "answer": "To mitigate the threat of standoff ballistic attacks against critical substation assets, the facility must deploy specialized ballistic-resistant perimeter walls or enclosure barriers certified to UL 752 Level 8 or Level 10 standards, or complying with NIJ Level IV specifications. These barriers typically consist of heavy-gauge structural steel panels, composite armor matrices, or thick pre-cast reinforced concrete walls engineered to stop armor-piercing rifle rounds (such as .30-06 AP). The configuration must completely obscure the line of sight to the transformers from external high points, incorporate thermal management ventilation to prevent equipment overheating, and be placed at a distance that accounts for spall and kinetic energy transfer, ensuring the transformer casing remains unpenetrated.",
