    "id": 141,
    "question": "A multinational technology enterprise implements a comprehensive background screening policy for all global personnel. During a routine re-investigation of an executive with access to proprietary algorithmic code, the background check company discovers a foreign criminal conviction for financial fraud that occurred five years ago but was omitted from the executive's original application. Under US corporate governance frameworks and internal risk mitigation models, what sequential steps must the security director execute?",
    "answer": "The security director must first immediately restrict the executive’s logical and physical access privileges to all proprietary code bases and sensitive networks to mitigate immediate insider threat risk. Next, the director must notify corporate legal counsel and human resources to review the discrepancy under corporate compliance guidelines. Because this involves an omission of material facts and a financial fraud conviction, an internal administrative review must be launched. The security director must ensure that any formal employment action complies with the FCRA (pre-adverse action notice if an external consumer report was used) and evaluate whether the executive's actions have already resulted in data compromise by initiating a retrospective digital forensic audit of all systems the executive accessed during their tenure.",
