    "id": 142,
    "question": "A high-security pharmaceutical warehouse utilizes an active infrared (AIR) perimeter beam system paired with a pan-tilt-zoom (PTZ) camera network for automated alarm assessment. During heavy fog conditions, the AIR system suffers from severe signal attenuation, resulting in a continuous stream of nuisance alarms. Concurrently, the fog renders the standard optical PTZ cameras ineffective for target identification. What technical modifications and alternative sensor technologies must be deployed to restore situational awareness?",
    "answer": "To restore situational awareness in adverse environmental conditions like heavy fog, the facility must replace or supplement the AIR beams with a perimeter intrusion detection system (PIDS) that is unaffected by atmospheric visibility, such as a fence-mounted fiber-optic microphonic cable system or a ground-based FMCW (Frequency Modulated Continuous Wave) radar array. For the assessment network, the standard optical CCTV cameras must be supplemented with long-wave infrared (LWIR) thermal imaging cameras. Thermal cameras detect the thermal radiation emitted by objects rather than relying on visible light reflections, allowing them to penetrate thick fog, smoke, and complete darkness to provide clear, high-contrast imagery of human intruders for security operators.",
