    "id": 143,
    "question": "A corporate campus undergoes a security audit of its emergency evacuation plans. The auditor flags that the facility’s assembly points for a post-bomb threat evacuation are located in the open parking lot directly visible from the main public street. From an explosive threat and counter-terrorism perspective, what specific hazard does this assembly layout present, and where should alternative assembly points be established?",
    "answer": "This layout creates extreme exposure to a secondary attack vector, such as a secondary improvised explosive device (IED) pre-staged in the parking lot or a drive-by active shooter engagement targeting the gathered crowd. A fundamental principle of bomb incident counter-measures is that evacuation assembly points must be located in areas that offer physical protection (cover and concealment) from the primary blast zone and are completely secure from secondary ambushes. Alternative assembly points should be established upwind and uphill at a safe distance (minimum 300 to 500 feet for standard briefcase-sized threats, larger for VBIED profiles), ideally behind solid structural barriers or inside secondary hardened buildings that prevent direct line-of-sight exposure from public roads.",
