    "id": 144,
    "question": "An asset protection professional is establishing an international supply chain security program aligned with the Customs-Trade Partnership Against Terrorism (C-TPAT) framework. When auditing an overseas manufacturing and shipping facility, the professional notes that shipping containers are sealed using standard plastic indicative seals. What specific regulatory non-compliance does this present, and what physical standard must the container seals satisfy to meet C-TPAT mandates?",
    "answer": "Plastic indicative seals are a direct violation of C-TPAT security criteria. To comply with C-TPAT and international supply chain security mandates, all maritime and cross-border shipping containers must be secured using high-security bolt or cable seals that meet or exceed the ISO 17712 standard for \"High Security Seals.\" These seals are engineered to resist aggressive physical tampering, require specialized heavy-duty bolt cutters to remove, and display unique, unalterable serial numbering. The security protocol must also mandate the \"VVTT\" inspection process (Verify seal number, Visually inspect seal condition, Tug on the seal to ensure proper seating, and Twist the seal to detect tampering) at every transfer point in the supply chain.",
