    "question": "A high-containment biomedical research facility handling Select Agents and Toxins must design a security program to counter the threat of an insider attempting to exfiltrate biological pathogens. The facility utilizes electronic access control, biometric tracking within laboratories, and a strict inventory management protocol. If an authorized researcher attempts to remove a viral culture vial by concealing it within a personal item, what technological control operating at the exit portal acts as the final line of physical interception?",
    "answer": "The final line of physical interception at the exit portal must consist of a mandatory, single-occupancy security mantrap equipped with specialized chemical, biological, or radiological detection sensors alongside an automated container inspection system (such as a high-resolution cabinet X-ray or millimeter-wave screening system) capable of imaging concealed personal property. Furthermore, the laboratory's inventory verification protocol must utilize real-time radio-frequency identification (RFID) or smart-container tracking arrays that link directly to the access control database. If a pathogen vial is removed from its designated storage containment unit without an authorized electronic transport manifest, the access control system automatically locks down the exit portals of the laboratory zone, preventing the researcher from exiting into public corridors until a physical response force arrives on-site. Here is the next set of 50 highly complex, scenario-based flashcard questions and deep explanations for the ASIS Certified Protection Professional (CPP) exam, numbered 151 through 200.",
