    "id": 153,
    "question": "An asset protection professional is assessing risk for an offshore oil platform in the Gulf of Mexico under the Maritime Transportation Security Act (MTSA). The platform’s single loss expectancy (SLE) for a catastrophic cyber-physical sabotage event is calculated at $\\$250,000,000$. If implementing an industrial control system (ICS) network segmentation architecture reduces the annualized rate of occurrence (ARO) from 0.08 to 0.02, what is the maximum annual cost of this control that yields a positive return on security investment (ROSI)?",
    "answer": "First, calculate the baseline Annualized Loss Expectancy: $ALE_{\\text{baseline}} = SLE \\times ARO_{\\text{baseline}} = \\$250,000,000 \\times 0.08 = \\$20,000,000$. Next, calculate the mitigated Annualized Loss Expectancy: $ALE_{\\text{mitigated}} = SLE \\times ARO_{\\text{mitigated}} = \\$250,000,000 \\times 0.02 = \\$5,000,000$. The annual risk reduction value is the difference between the two: $\\$20,000,000 - \\$5,000,000 = \\$15,000,000$. To maintain a positive return on security investment, the total annual cost of operating and maintaining the network segmentation architecture must be strictly less than $\\$15,000,000$. Any expenditure exceeding this threshold would cost more than the financial loss it prevents.",
