    "id": 155,
    "question": "A regional healthcare network must protect its patient database to comply with the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) Security Rule (45 CFR Part 164). A system architect proposes deploying a centralized biometric database to verify the identity of medical personnel accessing electronic protected health information (ePHI). What specific administrative and technical safeguards must be implemented to manage the risk of a biometric data breach?",
    "answer": "Biometric data (such as iris patterns or fingerprint templates) cannot be rotated, changed, or reissued if compromised, creating unique long-term identity theft risks. Under HIPAA administrative safeguards, the healthcare network must conduct a formal Risk Analysis to document threat vectors targeting the biometric server. Technical safeguards require that the raw biometric images never be stored. Instead, they must be converted into encrypted, irreversibly mathematical hashes at the edge device before transmission. The database containing these templates must utilize AES-256 encryption at rest, operate on a segmented network completely isolated from the public internet, and incorporate strict role-based access control (RBAC) backed by continuous cryptographic logging to detect unauthorized administrative queries.",
