    "id": 156,
    "question": "A structural engineer is designing a perimeter retaining wall for a high-value government facility to protect against vehicle-borne improvised explosive devices (VBIEDs). The design utilizes a reinforced concrete cantilever wall. If the threat profile specifies a blast overpressure wave generated by a 1,000-pound TNT equivalent explosion detonating at a standoff distance of 50 feet, what structural dynamic behavior must the wall exhibit to prevent catastrophic fragmentation or collapse into the facility?",
    "answer": "The wall must be engineered to undergo controlled plastic deformation rather than brittle failure under dynamic impulsive loading. This requires a high-ductility reinforced concrete design conforming to American Concrete Institute (ACI) 318 standards for seismic and blast resistance. The reinforcing steel must be closely spaced, continuous, and properly anchored with 135-degree seismic hooks to tie the wall to its footing. This ensures that when the high-pressure shockwave hits, the steel absorbs the kinetic energy by yielding elastically and plastically. This mechanism dampens the blast energy, controls structural cracking, and prevents the concrete from spalling off the inner face as lethal high-velocity fragments.",
