    "id": 157,
    "question": "An enterprise security team is designing an emergency egress control system for a manufacturing plant where hazardous chemical gasses are utilized. The physical security plan includes electromagnetic locks on all major zone boundary doors. To satisfy the safety requirements of the International Fire Code (IFC) and OSHA, what mechanical and electronic overrides must be present at each door to guarantee egress if the automated fire alarm system interface fails?",
    "answer": "Relying solely on a software or fire alarm relay interface to unlock electromagnetic doors is a severe life safety violation, as those relays can fuse or fail during an incident. To satisfy the IFC and OSHA, each door secured by an electromagnetic lock must incorporate two independent, redundant physical overrides. First, an approved manual touch-bar or latch mechanism integrated into the door hardware must mechanically disrupt power directly to the magnet when pressure is applied. Second, an emergency break-glass or push-button station (clearly labeled \"Emergency Door Release\") must be mounted on the egress side 40 to 48 inches above the floor, wired in series directly with the lock’s primary power supply wires, ensuring that physically pushing the button breaks the circuit and drops power to the magnet immediately.",
