    "question": "A secure warehouse facility stores high-value microprocessors. The perimeter is protected by a continuous 8-foot chain-link fence. The security director wants to implement a perimeter intrusion detection system (PIDS) utilizing a microphonic coaxial cable system. If the local environment experiences high-frequency acoustic noise from an adjacent commercial airport, how must the system's digital signal processor (DSP) be configured to minimize false and nuisance alarms?",
    "answer": "High-frequency acoustic noise and vibrations from low-flying aircraft can cause widespread false alarms on microphonic fence cables if the system is improperly tuned. The digital signal processor (DSP) must be configured using advanced frequency-domain filtering and signature analysis algorithms. The DSP must apply a band-pass filter to attenuate the specific low-frequency rumbles and high-frequency acoustic signatures characteristic of jet engines. Concurrently, the alarm logic must require a specific spatial and temporal pattern: it must detect localized, high-amplitude mechanical impacts or continuous scraping frequencies (indicative of cutting or climbing) within a tight time window, while rejecting uniform, widespread vibration signals that register across the entire cable length simultaneously.",
