    "id": 160,
    "question": "A banking institution operates an integrated security operations center (ISOC) that monitors both physical security telemetry and cybersecurity event streams. A security analyst notices a physical access control alert showing an employee badge being swiped at a critical records vault in New York. Exactly 4 minutes later, the same employee badge is used to log into a network terminal at a branch office in London. What specific security anomaly does this represent, and what automated response protocol should the ISOC trigger?",
    "answer": "This anomaly represents an \"impossible travel\" or velocity violation, indicating a high-probability credential compromise or badge-cloning event, as a human being cannot physically traverse the distance between New York and London in 4 minutes. The automated response protocol must initiate an immediate dual-lockdown state. The identity access management (IAM) system must terminate the active network session in London and lock the user's active directory account. Simultaneously, the physical security system must flag the physical badge as compromised, revoking its access rights globally, while alerting the physical security force at both the New York and London facilities to respond to the respective locations to identify and intercept the individuals attempting to use the credentials.",
