    "id": 161,
    "question": "A chemical refinement plant must comply with the Maritime Transportation Security Act (MTSA) Facility Security Plan rules. The facility security officer (FSO) must establish a declaration of security (DoS) protocol with arriving commercial vessels. What specific operational conditions mandate the execution of a formal DoS between the facility and the vessel’s master, and what is its legal purpose under US Coast Guard regulations?",
    "answer": "Under US Coast Guard regulations (33 CFR Part 105), a Declaration of Security (DoS) must be executed when the facility and the vessel are operating at different Maritime Security (MarSec) Levels, when either the facility or the vessel is operating at MarSec Level 2 or 3, when a cruise ship or a vessel carrying certain dangerous cargoes (CDC) interfaces with the facility, or when specifically directed by the local Captain of the Port. The legal purpose of the DoS is to establish a binding agreement that clearly delineates the specific security responsibilities, communication protocols, and coordinated protective measures that both the facility and the vessel will execute during the port interface to mitigate the risk of a transportation security incident.",
