    "id": 163,
    "question": "A financial institution experiences a ransomware attack that encrypts its primary loan processing architecture. The threat actors demand a $\\$2,000,000$ payment in cryptocurrency to provide the decryption key. Before advising senior leadership on whether to authorize a payment, the corporate security director must consult with legal counsel to evaluate compliance with what specific US Department of the Treasury regulatory agency framework?",
    "answer": "The security director must consult with legal counsel to evaluate the situation against the regulations administered by the Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC). OFAC maintains the Specially Designated Nationals and Blocked Persons (SDN) List. Under US law, facilitating, processing, or making a ransomware payment to an entity or individual listed on the SDN list, or located in a comprehensively sanctioned jurisdiction (e.g., Iran, North Korea, Russia-linked threat groups), is strictly prohibited and can result in severe civil and criminal penalties for the corporation and its executives under strict liability frameworks, regardless of whether the company knew the true identity of the ransomware actor.",
