    "id": 355,
    "question": "A critical infrastructure chemical facility covered by the Chemical Facility Anti-Terrorism Standards (CFATS) needs to upgrade its perimeter delay mechanisms to meet Risk-Based Performance Standards (RBPS) 1 and 2. The facility faces a specific threat of forced vehicular entry by a heavy truck. How should the security manager calculate and integrate K-rated (or ASTM F2656) barriers into the existing physical security layer?",
    "answer": "The security manager must select barriers rated under ASTM F2656 (which replaced the older K-rating system) based on the facility's specific design basis threat. For a heavy medium-duty truck (15,000 lbs) traveling at 50 mph, an M50 rating with a penetration rating of P1 (less than 3.3 feet of penetration) must be selected. These active or passive bollards must be placed at the final denial line, integrated with engineered approach roadways that force vehicles to slow down via sharp turns or chicanes, ensuring that the kinetic energy of an approaching threat cannot bypass or overwhelm the barrier's structural foundations.",
