    "question": "Evaluate the legal and operational implications of adopting a \"hands-off\" non-intervention asset protection policy versus an active physical intervention policy for contract security guards responding to high-value retail distribution center cargo thefts in a high-crime US jurisdiction.",
    "answer": "A non-intervention policy drastically reduces corporate exposure to third-party civil liability, negligent retention lawsuits, and worker's compensation claims stemming from guard injuries or accidental bystander harm. However, it increases direct asset loss, degrades the deterrent value of the security presence, and can invalidate certain inland marine insurance policies that require active protection of transit goods. Conversely, an active physical intervention policy enhances asset recovery and deters sophisticated threat actors, but it elevates corporate liability to astronomical levels under tort law concepts of vicarious liability and respondeat superior. This necessitates rigorous, continuous training on the use-of-force continuum and defensible documentation protocols to survive intense civil litigation.",
