    "question": "In corporate financial investigations within the United States, how does the Federal Rules of Evidence (FRE) Rule 502 protect attorney-client privilege and work-product doctrine during the inadvertent disclosure of sensitive electronic security audit reports to a regulatory agency?",
    "answer": "FRE Rule 502 dictates that an inadvertent disclosure of privileged corporate security data or audit reports in a federal proceeding or to a federal agency does not operate as a widespread subject-matter waiver of the privilege if the holder of the privilege took reasonable steps to prevent disclosure and promptly took reasonable steps to rectify the error under the \"clawback\" provisions. The security manager and legal counsel must document that a comprehensive pre-production review was performed and that an immediate written demand was issued to the receiving agency upon discovering the inadvertent transmission, thereby preserving the confidentiality of the remaining non-disclosed investigative files.",
