    "question": "During an active workplace violence incident involving a disgruntled employee at a multi-story US corporate headquarters, the enterprise security operations center (GSOC) loses access to all real-time closed-circuit television (CCTV) feeds due to an automated network security lockdown. What alternative intelligence-gathering and communication protocols must the GSOC supervisor deploy to guide tactical law enforcement responders effectively?",
    "answer": "The GSOC supervisor must immediately shift from visual verification to acoustic and system-based tracking protocols. This involves monitoring real-time electronic access control logs to see which badge readers are registering forced entries or valid rejections, tracking mass notification system response metrics (where employees report their safe or compromised status via two-way mobile apps), and analyzing cellular tower or Wi-Fi access point connection drops within the facility. This aggregated telemetry must be funneled directly to the responding law enforcement Incident Commander via dedicated radio interoperability channels or tactical data terminals to paint an analytical picture of the active shooter’s trajectory through the building.",
