    "question": "Critically analyze the structural and legal challenges of implementing a biometric-only access control system (e.g., facial recognition or fingerprint) across a diverse US workforce, factoring in EEOC guidelines and ADA compliance.",
    "answer": "Biometric-only access systems present substantial legal vulnerabilities under Title VII of the Civil Rights Act and the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA). Certain biometric technologies exhibit systemic demographic biases or fail to read individuals with specific physical disabilities or skin conditions, leading to discriminatory access delays or denials. Furthermore, religious objections to biometric enrollment must be legally accommodated by the employer under EEOC mandates. From an operational risk standpoint, biometric data cannot be changed if breached, unlike a password or smart card; a compromised biometric database creates severe, permanent liability for the enterprise under state-specific privacy acts like Illinois' BIPA. Organizations must mitigate these risks by offering multi-factor authentication (MFA) alternatives, such as smart cards combined with PINs, as non-discriminatory backup modalities.",
