    "question": "Define the legal doctrine of \"Negligent Security\" within US premises liability tort law, and identify the primary element a plaintiff must establish to win a civil lawsuit against a commercial property owner following a violent third-party criminal assault.",
    "answer": "Negligent security is a subset of premises liability law holding property owners or occupiers civilly liable for criminal acts committed by third parties against visitors if the owner failed to provide adequate security measures. The primary, most heavily litigated element the plaintiff must prove is \"foreseeability.\" The plaintiff must demonstrate that the property owner had actual or constructive knowledge that violent criminal acts were likely to occur on the premises, typically established by analyzing historical local crime statistics (Part 1 crimes), police call logs, and documentation of prior similar incidents on or immediately adjacent to that specific property.",
