    "question": "A security executive needs to draft a Master Security Plan for a new corporate high-rise in an active seismic and hurricane zone in the United States. How must the physical security design incorporate the conflicting requirements of NFPA 101 (Life Safety Code) and asset protection standards regarding emergency egress doors?",
    "answer": "The design must utilize delayed-egress locking systems or fail-safe electromagnetic locks that strictly comply with NFPA 101 mandates. In a non-emergency state, these doors remain secured from the outside to prevent unauthorized entry. However, upon activation of the building's fire alarm system, loss of primary power, or a verified seismic event detected by building automation sensors, the locks must automatically release to allow immediate, unhindered egress. For delayed-egress setups, pushing against the panic bar can delay opening by a maximum of 15 to 30 seconds while sounding a local alarm, allowing corporate security personnel to intercept unauthorized exits or asset theft while preserving life-safety compliance.",
