    "answer": "Traditional ROI is an ineffective metric for security operations because security is a loss-prevention function that does not generate direct revenue, often resulting in a negative or zero value on a standard balance sheet. Utilizing a Return on Prevention (ROP) or Cost-Benefit Analysis (CBA) framework allows the security executive to present upgrades in terms of risk reduction, liability avoidance, and operational continuity. By quantifying the annualized loss expectancy (ALE) before and after the proposed security controls, the security leader can demonstrate how the capital expenditure directly offsets potential catastrophic expenses, such as regulatory fines, legal settlements, business downtime, and insurance premium escalations, aligning security with corporate risk management objectives.",
