    "answer": "Defense-in-Depth is a structured security design philosophy that implements multiple, successive layers of physical and technical security controls so that an adversary must overcome each layer sequentially to reach a protected asset, thereby maximizing delay, detection, and response capabilities. The five foundational concentric layers, moving from the exterior inward, are: the boundary/property line perimeter layer (fencing, gates, LPRs), the clear zone/grounds layer (lighting, open terrain, vehicle barriers), the building exterior/facade layer (hardened doors, glazing, structural walls, intrusion sensors), the interior space/common area layer (access control readers, interior CCTV, motion detectors), and the asset-specific/vault layer (safes, high-security cages, strict multi-factor authentication zones).",
