    "question": "A US retail chain experiences a series of organized retail crime (ORC) raids where flash mobs breach stores simultaneously to steal high-value assets. How should the director of asset protection structure a multi-site physical security and intelligence framework to counter this operational pattern?",
    "answer": "The director must establish a centralized regional intelligence hub that aggregates OSINT data, social media monitoring feeds, and shared law enforcement bulletins to spot the pre-coordination markers of flash mob activity. At the store level, physical security controls must include installing high-shatter-resistance security glazing or heavy-duty motorized roll-down grilles at entrances that can be activated instantly via wireless panic buttons by employees. Security personnel must be trained to shift immediately to a defensive posture focused on life safety and asset containment—such as triggering automated locking mechanisms on high-value displays—while the centralized hub routes digital store video feeds directly to local police dispatchers to facilitate rapid apprehension away from the store floor.",
