    "question": "Define the legal protections and operational limits established by the US Federal Wiretap Act (Title III of the Omnibus Crime Control and Safe Streets Act) regarding corporate intercept of employee electronic communications on company-owned infrastructure.",
    "answer": "The Federal Wiretap Act strictly prohibits the intentional interception of wire, oral, or electronic communications without a judicial warrant or the prior consent of at least one party to the communication. For US corporations to legally monitor and intercept employee electronic communications (such as emails, network traffic, or phone calls) on company-owned systems without violating federal law, they must rely on two primary statutory exceptions: the \"ordinary course of business\" exception (providing the monitoring has a legitimate business purpose and utilizes company-provided equipment) and the \"consent\" exception. To establish defensible consent, the corporation must enforce explicit, mandatory electronic banners and signed user agreements detailing that employees have zero expectation of privacy when utilizing corporate networks, devices, and infrastructure.",
