    "id": 398,
    "question": "Critically evaluate the systemic risks, architectural changes, and operational advantages of migrating an enterprise infrastructure from a traditional Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) smart card credential system (e.g., PIV/CAC) to a FIDO2/WebAuthn passwordless authentication ecosystem for physical and logical access control.",
    "answer": "Traditional PKI smart cards provide exceptionally robust, cryptographically backed multi-factor authentication, but they carry massive operational overhead, requiring physical card printing facilities, specialized card readers at every endpoint and door, and complex certificate revocation list (CRL) management infrastructure. FIDO2/WebAuthn passwordless authentication removes this centralized credential management burden by utilizing asymmetric cryptography bound directly to user endpoints (such as mobile devices or security keys) via local biometric validation. This completely eliminates credential-phishing vectors and credential-stuffing attacks. However, integrating FIDO2 into physical access control systems (PACS) requires a major architectural overhaul, as legacy door controllers lack the native web-browser capabilities or API protocols needed to negotiate WebAuthn handshakes. Enterprises must deploy specialized smart readers and updated middleware capable of bridging FIDO2 tokens with standard physical access security protocols, creating a significant initial capital expenditure and introducing new dependency risks regarding mobile device operating system updates and cloud identity provider availability.",
