    "question": "Define the legal concept of \"Spoliation of Evidence\" within the US Federal Rules of Civil Procedure (FRCP), and explain its consequences for a corporate security department that fails to preserve video surveillance footage following a serious workplace injury.",
    "answer": "Spoliation of evidence is the intentional, reckless, or negligent destruction, alteration, or concealment of evidence relevant to ongoing or reasonably foreseeable litigation. Under FRCP Rule 37(e), if a corporate security department fails to take reasonable steps to preserve digital video surveillance footage following a significant incident, and that footage cannot be restored or replaced, the court can impose severe sanctions. These consequences include ordering monetary penalties, instructing the jury to presume that the destroyed evidence was unfavorable to the corporation (an \"adverse inference instruction\"), or, in extreme cases of intentional suppression, entering a default judgment against the corporation, resulting in an automatic loss of the civil lawsuit.",
