    "question": "A corporate security director at a US defense aerospace manufacturing plant discovers an undocumented, active network bridge connecting a critical automated CNC milling machine on the secure factory floor to an unauthorized commercial cellular hotspot hidden in an drop ceiling. What immediate operational containment and investigative isolation sequence must be executed to identify the insider threat without dropping volatile digital evidence or disrupting the production of defense assets?",
    "answer": "The security director must not immediately rip down or disconnect the rogue cellular hotspot, as doing so alerts the adversary and destroys volatile RAM data, active session states, and network routing logs. Instead, the director must quietly deploy an inline network tap or a managed switch port mirror on the CNC machine's network drop to capture all inbound and outbound packet traffic for forensic analysis. Simultaneously, covert physical and video surveillance must be directed at the drop-ceiling location to catch whoever services or retrieves the device. After capturing sufficient packet telemetry to determine what data was exfiltrated and identifying the command-and-control server IP, the security team must coordinate an off-shift physical containment: forensically cloning the device’s internal storage in situ if possible, and then executing a controlled logical isolation at the primary enterprise firewall, all while maintaining strict chain of custody for potential prosecution under the Defend Trade Secrets Act (DTSA).",
