    "id": 403,
    "question": "Analyze the risk exposure and operational resilience profiles of a cloud-native, decentralized Access Control System utilizing edge-intelligent IP controllers versus a traditional centralized on-premises architecture when subjected to a sustained distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attack targeting the corporate wide-area network (WAN).",
    "answer": "A centralized on-premises architecture relies entirely on local local-area network (LAN) infrastructure; if the external WAN is hit by a DDoS attack, local door controllers continue to communicate flawlessly with the on-site server, preserving real-time global event logging, badge provisioning, and administrative control. However, a cloud-native architecture becomes blind at the administrative level when the WAN link fails. While edge-intelligent IP controllers mitigate this by utilizing local non-volatile memory caches to make independent grant/deny decisions for existing users, they cannot process new visitor requests, update revocation lists (CRLs), or transmit emergency panic or duress signals back to a centralized Global Security Operations Center (GSOC). If the edge controller's internal buffer fills its memory capacity during a prolonged WAN outage, older transactional logs will be overwritten, resulting in a permanent loss of forensic audit trails and creating a compliance gap under frameworks like SOC 2 or NERC CIP.",
