    "answer": "Deploying armed personnel significantly enhances the visible deterrent profile and provides an immediate, lethal response capability against violent, armed takeover attempts, effectively lowering the risk of catastrophic asset loss. However, it exponentially inflates the corporation's civil liability exposure. Under the doctrine of respondeat superior, the corporate client can be held vicariously liable for a contract guard’s negligent or improper use of deadly force. Even if the jurisdiction recognizes the Castle Doctrine, that legal defense primarily shields individuals in their dwellings, not corporate entities or contract guards exercising force over property. An unjustified shooting will result in multi-million-dollar lawsuits for wrongful death and negligent training. Unarmed guards drastically lower this civil litigation risk profile and shift the focus to observation and early law enforcement notification, but they leave the facility vulnerable to armed criminal syndicates, potentially resulting in complete asset compromise and a failure to meet minimum insurance underwriting mandates for high-value cargo protection.",
