    "id": 409,
    "question": "Analyze how the implementation of User and Entity Behavior Analytics (UEBA) integrated with an automated Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response (SOAR) playbook changes the risk profile of internal intellectual property theft compared to traditional rule-based Data Loss Prevention (DLP) systems.",
    "answer": "Traditional rule-based DLP systems rely on static, known signatures or explicit file labeling; they are highly vulnerable to sophisticated insiders who can bypass controls by slowly trickle-downloading data, changing file extensions, or taking screenshots of sensitive data. UEBA transforms this dynamic by establishing a statistical baseline of normal behavioral patterns for every individual user (e.g., typical access times, data volumes, peer-group comparisons). When an insider deviates from this baseline—such as downloading an unusual volume of files at 3:00 AM—the SOAR playbook can execute immediate, micro-targeted mitigation, such as revoking the user's active network session, locking their physical access control badge, and alerting the insider threat team within milliseconds. While this drastically reduces the time to detect and contain a breach, it introduces new risks: false-positive automated lockouts of critical executives during high-stress corporate transactions can cause severe business disruption, and continuous behavioral profiling can trigger employee grievances or legal challenges regarding workplace privacy laws if not governed by clear, transparent corporate policies.",
