    "question": "A defense contractor facility must protect a room containing classified hardware from unauthorized visual and electronic eavesdropping through a large exterior window. How should the physical security engineer specify the window glazing, radio-frequency (RF) attenuation coatings, and acoustic masking systems to comply with ICD 705 standards?",
    "answer": "The engineer must specify a multi-layered, high-security glazing system. To combat forced entry and ballistic threats, the window must use UL 752 Level 3 or higher polycarbonate laminate. To prevent electronic and RF exfiltration, the glass must be treated with a high-performance, indium tin oxide (ITO) or silver-based thin-film coating that provides at least 40 dB to 60 dB of RF attenuation across a frequency range from 10 MHz to 10 GHz. To defeat acoustic eavesdropping via laser microphones or parabolic directional listening devices, the engineer must install transducers directly onto the window pane connected to an active white-noise or pink-noise acoustic masking generator, which introduces random vibrations into the glass to scramble the audio intelligibility of internal conversations. Finally, motorized, RF-shielded blackout blinds must be deployed to eliminate any direct line of sight from the exterior during classified operations.",
