    "question": "Critically analyze the security vulnerabilities inherent in utilizing standard 125 kHz Proximity cards versus 13.56 MHz MIFARE DESFire EV3 smart cards within an enterprise physical access control system, focusing on the mathematical and practical mechanics of exploit vectors.",
    "answer": "Legacy 125 kHz proximity cards (such as standard HID Prox) possess zero cryptographic security; they transmit their unique facility code and card identification number in the clear via unencrypted, unauthenticated radio frequency broadcasts. Anyone with a low-cost, readily available handheld device (like a Proxmark3 or a Flipper Zero) can clone the card from several inches away in seconds without the user's knowledge, allowing an adversary to replay the signal and gain illicit access to the facility. Conversely, 13.56 MHz MIFARE DESFire EV3 cards utilize advanced open cryptographic standards, implementing 128-bit AES encryption with a hardware random number generator to execute a mutual three-pass authentication handshake between the card and the reader before any card data is transmitted. This mathematically neutralizes sniffing, cloning, and man-in-the-middle replay attacks. Migrating to DESFire EV3 eliminates the vulnerability of credential cloning but requires a complete replacement of legacy readers, firmware upgrades across the entire controller panel architecture, and careful key-management governance, as losing the master cryptographic deployment keys can permanently brick the entire card fleet.",
