    "question": "A corporate campus is hit by an active shooter who is moving through an open-plan office environment. The facility uses an automated voice-evacuation system integrated with its fire alarm. How should the security director alter the automated life-safety messaging systems in real time to prevent casualties?",
    "answer": "The security director must immediately override the automated fire evacuation voice alarms. Standard fire alarms instruct occupants to exit the building via designated stairwells and common corridors; during an active shooter event, following these routes can herd large numbers of panicked employees directly into the shooter’s line of fire or choke points. The director must manually switch the mass notification system to broadcast pre-recorded or live tactical active shooter instructions emphasizing the \"Run, Hide, Fight\" protocol. The messaging must instruct occupants to secure themselves in offices, barricade doors, silence cell phones, and remain hidden unless they have a completely clear, verified path to an exit away from the reported location of the threat.",
