    "answer": "A high-profile overt footprint utilizes visible armed agents, marked support vehicles, formalized motorcade formations, and physical crowd-control barriers. This creates a powerful psychological deterrent that discourages opportunistic threat actors and establishes clear physical control over the environment. However, it attracts significant media attention, limits the principal's mobility, and can provoke hostile elements by projecting an image of arrogance or fear. A low-profile covert footprint uses plainclothes agents who blend seamlessly into the crowd, utilizing unvetted vehicles and relying heavily on advanced counter-surveillance, pre-incident route intelligence, and evasion tactics. This allows the principal to move naturally without drawing public scrutiny, significantly reducing the probability of a planned attack by keeping the principal's movements hidden. The strategic vulnerability of the covert approach is that if a security breach or active attack occurs, the detail lacks the immediate physical mass, vehicle armor, and firepower to suppress the threat instantly, relying entirely on rapid evacuation and luck to survive the initial engagement.",
