    "question": "Under the Sarbanes-Oxley Act (SOX) Section 302, what specific personal certifications must the Chief Executive Officer (CEO) and Chief Financial Officer (CFO) execute regarding corporate internal controls, and how does this impact the corporate security director's asset protection reporting mandates?",
    "answer": "SOX Section 302 mandates that the CEO and CFO must personally sign certifications in each quarterly and annual financial report stating that they have reviewed the reports, that the reports contain no material misstatements, and that they are personally responsible for establishing, maintaining, and reviewing the effectiveness of internal disclosure controls and procedures. This directly impacts the corporate security director because physical and logical security systems protect the enterprise databases where financial data is generated and stored. The security director must provide formal, auditable assurances and reports to executive leadership proving that physical access controls to data centers, asset tracking systems, and fraud prevention measures are fully functional and free of material weaknesses, as any unaddressed security vulnerability can expose the executives to severe criminal penalties for signing inaccurate compliance statements.",
