    "id": 488,
    "question": "A private corporate security officer detains an employee for questioning regarding suspected tool theft. The officer locks the employee in an office, denies their request to leave, and falsely states that the police are outside waiting to arrest them if they do not sign a confession. Analyze the potential civil liabilities under tort law for both the security officer and the employer.",
    "answer": "The security officer’s actions create direct liability for several intentional torts. First, locking the employee in a room and denying their freedom of movement without legal authorization constitutes false imprisonment. Second, falsely threatening immediate police arrest to coerce a confession constitutes intentional infliction of emotional distress (IIED) and civil coercion. Because the security officer is an agent of the employer acting within the course and scope of their employment, the corporation faces absolute vicarious liability under the doctrine of respondeat superior. Any confession obtained under these conditions is completely legally void and would serve as primary evidence against the corporation in a multi-million-dollar civil lawsuit for wrongful detention and civil rights violations.",
