    "id": 501,
    "question": "A security executive is reviewing an outsourcing contract for an enterprise video surveillance cloud platform. The vendor includes a limitation of liability clause capping their total financial exposure at $50,000 in the event of system downtime. The security executive knows a system failure during a major security breach could cost the company millions. Apply corporate risk management principles to restructure this clause to protect the organization.",
    "answer": "A $50,000 cap is entirely inadequate for an enterprise-class dependency. The security executive must reject the flat cap and negotiate a multi-tiered liability structure. First, tie the limitation of liability to a multiple of the annual contract value (e.g., 3x to 5x the annual recurring revenue paid to the vendor), which significantly scales up the recovery pool. Second, negotiate a carve-out (complete exclusion) from the limitation of liability cap for incidents resulting from the vendor’s gross negligence, willful misconduct, or breaches of data privacy and confidentiality obligations. This ensures that if a massive breach or prolonged outage is caused by the vendor completely failing to adhere to basic security standards, the corporation can sue for full consequential and compensatory damages without any pre-determined contractual limit. ASIS Certified Protection Professional (CPP) Flashcards (502–552) Target Era/Framework: 2026–2027 Exam Specifications / United States Legal & Regulatory Frameworks Difficulty Rating: 8.5+ / 10 (Complex conceptual synthesis, advanced application scenarios, and rigorous analytical evaluation across all 7 core domains)",
