    "question": "Analyze how the psychological framework of the Theory of Planned Behavior (Attitude, Subjective Norms, Perceived Behavioral Control) dictates the success of a corporate department's internal Compliance and Security Culture enforcement campaign.",
    "answer": "The Theory of Planned Behavior states that an individual's intention to execute a specific behavior is driven by three components: 1) Attitude: whether they view the security rule as valuable or a waste of time; 2) Subjective Norms: the perceived social pressure from peers and management to comply; and 3) Perceived Behavioral Control: how easy or difficult they find it to adhere to the rule. If a security department launches an awareness campaign that focuses exclusively on rules without addressing these psychological drivers, the campaign will fail. If employees perceive that executives routinely bypass security mantraps (Subjective Norms favor non-compliance) or find that accessing secure databases requires navigating an unmanageable sequence of login screens (Low Perceived Control), they will actively look for workarounds (e.g., propping doors open or writing down passwords). Success requires aligning all three vectors: ensuring leadership visibly models security behaviors, configuring technical controls to minimize operational friction (maximizing control), and continuously communicating why the protection mechanics matter to transform security into an organic cultural norm rather than a bureaucratic obstacle.",
    "topic": "Crisis Management & BCP"
