    "question": "Under the National Incident Management System (NIMS), what are the explicit criteria that differentiate an incident requiring a Type 5 Incident Management Team (IMT) from an incident necessitating a Type 1 IMT, and how do these designations alter resource procurement pathways?",
    "answer": "The classification of an incident from Type 5 to Type 1 is dictated by the complexity, geographic scope, hazard severity, and resource requirements of the event. A Type 5 Incident is a highly localized, routine event (such as a minor localized chemical spill or single-car accident) that requires minimal resources, involves 1-6 response personnel, handles the crisis within a few hours, and does not require a formal written Incident Action Plan (IAP). A Type 1 Incident is a catastrophic national-level crisis (such as a major Category 5 hurricane strike or a devastating large-scale terrorist attack) that requires thousands of response personnel, spans multiple operational periods over weeks or months, involves multi-jurisdictional Unified Command, and mandates a comprehensive, written IAP for every shift. This designation drastically alters resource procurement: Type 5 relies exclusively on local, pre-staged mutual-aid agreements, whereas Type 1 triggers formal federal emergency declarations under the Stafford Act, unlocking the direct logistics pipelines of FEMA, the National Guard, and federal funding networks.",
