    "question": "Analyze the strategic security trade-offs, operational failure modes, and systemic risks associated with transitioning an enterprise organization’s physical access control backbone from a traditional Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) framework to an Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) framework.",
    "answer": "A traditional RBAC framework assigns physical access privileges based entirely on static job roles (e.g., an employee labeled \"IT Engineer\" has implicit access to all server rooms 24/7). The strategic vulnerability is privilege creep and lack of granularity; engineers retain access even when off-duty or unassigned to a specific project. An ABAC framework evaluates dynamic, real-time attributes (User attributes: role, training certifications; Asset attributes: security clearance, sensitivity; Environmental attributes: current time, physical location, active threat level). Strategically, ABAC dramatically minimizes the attack surface by enforcing true least privilege. However, its operational failure mode is policy processing latency and structural complexity. If the connection between the physical access control panel and the identity evaluation engine suffers a micro-second delay or sync failure, the system fails to evaluate the attributes, defaulting to a fail-secure state that locks employees out of critical production zones during standard business cycles, transforming a security optimization into a operational continuity risk.",
