    "question": "An enterprise network experiences a coordinated Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) Attack where the adversary is utilizing ARP Cache Poisoning to spoof the MAC address of the primary default gateway, routing all outbound corporate internet traffic through a malicious interception proxy. Outline the immediate network configuration and layer-2 switch security controls that the network security engineer must deploy to terminate the attack and prevent future spoofing vectors.",
    "answer": "To immediately terminate the active MitM attack, the engineer must clear the poisoned cache tables by executing a global flush of the ARP tables across all affected endpoint workstations and network interfaces. To prevent future spoofing vectors, the engineer must deploy a robust layer-2 switch security control known as Dynamic ARP Inspection (DAI). DAI requires the concurrent activation of DHCP Snooping. DHCP Snooping builds a trusted database mapping valid IP addresses to verified physical MAC addresses as endpoints connect to the network. DAI leverages this database to inspect every single incoming ARP packet on untrusted switch ports; if an incoming ARP packet contains a MAC-to-IP binding that does not match the validated database entry (such as the adversary attempting to announce their MAC address as the default gateway), DAI instantly drops the packet and disables the switch port, neutralizing the layer-2 interception vector.",
